      "descriptions": [
        {
          "value": "Following his re-election in 1936, Franklin D. Roosevelt began a second presidential term on 20 January 1937, with John Nance Garner as vice-president. His first inauguration had taken place on 4 March 1933; the Twentieth Amendment changed the inauguration date for 1937 onward. Roosevelt communicated with the public through informal radio addresses. Early broadcasts discussed the banking crisis, the New Deal programme and economic recovery, followed by an address on public works and Social Security in 1935. His Good Neighbor Policy sought improved relations and trade with Latin America through a stated commitment to respect and non-intervention. The population figure shown separately is an earlier, geographically limited census observation, not a current-year estimate.",
          "validFrom": "1937-01-20",
          "validUntil": "1941-01-20",
          "sourceIds": [
