      "descriptions": [
        {
          "value": "After Hitler’s appointment as chancellor in January 1933, the Nazi leadership dismantled Germany’s democratic institutions and suppressed political opposition. The regime used emergency powers and legislation to concentrate authority, while excluding opponents from public life. Following President Hindenburg’s death in August 1934, Hitler combined presidential authority with the chancellorship. The Nazi Party dominated a dictatorship rather than a competitive parliamentary system. This overview describes the consolidated regime of the mid-1930s, without treating its later territorial conquests as facts about every selected year. The atlas’s boundary reconstructions are a separate geographical source and do not establish the constitutional or political status of every territory represented on the map.",
          "validFrom": "1934-08-02",
          "validUntil": "1939-01-01",
