      "descriptions": [
        {
          "value": "The Soviet Union was governed through a Communist Party dictatorship. By the mid-1930s, Joseph Stalin had eliminated major rivals and consolidated control over party and state. Forced collectivisation and centrally directed industrial development had imposed severe costs on the population. Political policing and repression were central instruments of government. During 1936–1938, public trials and mass persecution formed part of the Great Terror, which reached its height in 1937–1938. Leadership here refers to Stalin’s dominant position in the party-state system, rather than an assumed presidential or prime-ministerial office. The overview is limited to this period; it does not substitute information about the later Russian Federation for the Soviet political entity.",
