      "descriptions": [
        {
          "value": "The Meiji Constitution placed the emperor at the centre of the constitutional order. Its provisions described imperial sovereignty and provided for legislative power to be exercised with the Imperial Diet. The Diet consisted of a House of Peers and a House of Representatives. Ministers of state advised the emperor and bore responsibility for their advice. This dossier records those constitutional provisions rather than assuming that the formal document explains every aspect of political practice in the 1930s. Its curated coverage is limited to the imperial polity represented by the mapped identity. It does not use the post-war constitution or a modern government profile as a silent substitute for the selected historical period.",
