      "descriptions": [
        {
          "value": "The Government of India Act of 1858 transferred the East India Company’s administrative powers, possessions and armed forces to the British Crown. Responsibility in Britain passed to a Secretary of State for India, assisted by a council, while the Crown appointed the viceroy and governors. This arrangement is commonly described as the British Raj. It was an imperial system, rather than an independent national government. The mapped entity should not be read as proof of uniform direct British administration across all of the subcontinent. Princely states had a distinct position, and the geographical dataset’s authority fields provide only a simplified representation. This dossier keeps that distinction visible rather than treating administrative control as an unqualified statement of sovereignty.",
          "validFrom": "1858",
          "validUntil": "1947",
          "sourceIds": [
            "raj-parliament",
            "partition"
          ],
          "confidence": "documented"
        }
      ],
